<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Parkes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Henry Parkes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Parkes was an English-born journalist and politician who served five times as premier of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> between 1872 and 1891. He promoted free public education and free trade. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenterfield-oration">Tenterfield Oration</a> of 1889 launched the federation movement, earning him the name Father of Federation, although he died before the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> was created in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-parkes.jpg" alt="Henry Parkes" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryparkes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Parkes was born on 27 May 1815 at Canley, now part of Coventry, the son of a small tenant farmer, and received little formal schooling, educating himself by wide reading. On 11 July 1836 he married Clarinda Varney, a butcher's daughter to whom he had addressed poems. After losing their first two children they emigrated, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> on the Strathfieldsaye on 25 July 1839 with only a few shillings. He worked as a labourer on John Jamison's Regentville estate near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a>, then as a tide waiter in the Customs Department, and published his first volume of poems, Stolen Moments, in 1842.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined the movement for self-government, opposed the transportation of convicts and attended the protest at Circular Quay against a convict ship on 8 June 1849. In December 1850 he founded the Empire, which soon became a daily and criticised Governor Charles Augustus FitzRoy and William Wentworth's plan for an unelected upper house, though the paper never paid and he later went bankrupt with debts of £48,500. In parliament he backed free trade and immigration, and his Public Schools Act 1866 required teachers to be trained. After Henry James O'Farrell shot Alfred, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in 1868, Parkes pressed claims of a vast Fenian conspiracy, and when nothing more came of it he was accused of being anti-Irish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As premier he campaigned against Chinese immigration, saying he did so not because he thought the Chinese inferior but to preserve the type of his own nation. On 24 October 1889, at the Tenterfield School of Arts, he called for a convention to devise a federal constitution, and he proposed the name Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. In 1891 he rejected as barbarous a proposal to take the vote from Aboriginal people. Replaced by Reid as leader of the Free Trade Party, he sat as an independent and lost to Reid in Sydney-King in 1895. He died in poverty on 27 April 1896. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-deakin">Alfred Deakin</a> called him a large-brained self-educated Titan whose natural field was Parliament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Parkes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1880s concern about French and German colonies in the Pacific, Asian immigration and intercolonial trade encouraged the movement for federation, famously advanced by <b>Henry Parkes</b>, premier of New South Wales, in his Tenterfield Oration of 1889.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under premiers such as <b>Henry Parkes</b>, a leading advocate of federation, New South Wales joined the other colonies to form the Commonwealth of Australia on 1 January 1901, becoming a state.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.33,-1.58&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Warwickshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry Parkes"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 5653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 5653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.33,-1.58&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
